Reply 1: prompt editing (to Vyas, 12:15, and Giri's to-do)

On prompt editing: Memory Bank doesn't expose or let us edit its extraction prompt. What we can configure on the bank instead:

Memory topics: a label plus a description of what to extract for each. These act as the prompt. Built-in topics such as user preferences can also be turned on or off.
Few-shot examples: sample conversations with the memories we expect from them.
Extraction model: Gemini 2.5 is rejected; it requires gemini-3.5-flash, which our project can only reach at the global location.
Other settings: third-person phrasing, a consolidation setting, structured schemas, and time-to-live.

In the POC I put the "focus on durable shopping preferences, ignore one-off orders and medical conditions" instruction into the topic descriptions. It followed it well: no one-off details were kept. It still inferred "prefers heart-healthy breakfast options" from "my doctor said my cholesterol is high", though.

Prompt control by option:

A (custom batch): full control, since we own the prompt.
B (Memory Bank managed): no prompt; topics, examples and model choice only.
C (agent-level): full control, through the agent's instructions and the preference descriptions it's given.

This fits Vyas's decision: topics and examples are bank configuration, so no control plane is needed in between for prompt customization.

Reply 2: data for the decision (to Vyas, 12:28)

I ran Option B, Memory Bank's managed extraction, on a dedicated instance in dev: one synthetic customer, with planted preferences, changes and noise.

Single run (96 customer turns): 15 of 15 planted preferences captured, 0 of 5 one-off details kept, the milk-brand switch handled. Search p50 214 ms, p95 277 ms.

Three-round test (204 customer turns so far; round 3 still to run):

Explicit preferences: 9 of 9 captured. Implicit ones, shown only by repeated choices: 8 of 8.
Both preference changes handled (Chobani → Oikos; vegetarian Fridays → Mediterranean), with the old value marked as previous.
"Which yogurt should I recommend?" returned Oikos after the change.
204 turns became 20 facts, about 14:1 compression.

Cost (real token use from Cloud Monitoring): extraction reads about 21 tokens per conversation token, and its output is about 60% of its input, mostly reasoning. That's roughly $0.035 per session, or about $146 per 1M conversation tokens. Memory Bank's own read and write charges are negligible.

Watch-outs:

Extraction ran from 6 s to 16 minutes per session, with timeouts and throttling on 3.5 Flash, so it can only run in the background.
Broad questions like "all preferred brands" miss stored facts, because search returns only the closest few; profile-wide reads need a list of every fact.
Inferences can go beyond what the customer said; the health example is above.

Options A and C aren't measured yet. I can run the same synthetic conversations through C (agent-level extraction) to compare quality and cost directly.

