Points in the thread that still need an answer
Rahul, 12:35: "Can we enable Memory Bank in dev and do some POC…" You already have one: a dedicated POC instance in e2eml-222003, with the results above. Reply to that effect and offer to set up the platform project, since Rahul said he can't do it himself. Two setup gotchas to mention:
Vyas's curl creates the bank with an empty config, which means default topics. If anyone sets an extraction model, Gemini 2.5 is rejected and 3.5 Flash must use the global path.
Expect occasional timeouts and throttling; callers need retries.
Privacy allow list (about 35 types, from Rahul's note via Joe). This maps directly onto Memory Bank topics: one topic per approved type, and Memory Bank extracts only into configured topics. That is governance by configuration, as Pawan suggested. Two caveats:
Topics are set per bank (per scope keys), not per agent. A per-agent allow list needs separate scope-key configs or separate banks.
The deny list and anything in between can only be described in topic text. It isn't guaranteed, as the "heart-healthy" inference shows.
When a memory becomes available to another agent (Rahul: end of session; Vyas: a non-blocking call during the session). Our data supports end of session. Even a non-blocking call made during the session can take minutes to land: the median was about 2 minutes per session today, the worst 16.
Vyas's "do both". Saving immediately on "save" or "remember", plus offline processing of the session: Memory Bank supports both. Write an explicit fact directly, and run session extraction afterwards; both are in the SDK.
Cooklist's table of about 25 areas. That's the candidate topic list. Each area would need privacy approval (point 2), and areas such as "Dietary constraints" (allergies, medical restrictions) and "Household context" are the sensitive ones.
Giri's dimensions:
Implicit vs explicit: we now have data, both captured fully.
Sensitive vs non-sensitive: managed extraction has no sensitivity setting; the only lever is topic wording.
Text vs structured: the decision was "Text". Structured memory schemas also exist if attribute-level control is needed later.

